Let 
$f\left( x \right) = \left\{ {\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}
 {\frac{{3x + 4\tan x}}{x}}&{{\text{if}}}&{x \ne 0} \\ 
 k&{{\text{if}}}&{x = 0} 
\end{array}} \right.$ 
If $f$ is continuous at $x=0,$ then the value of $k$ is _______.

questions.s:
We have 
$\left| {{x^2} - 3x + 2} \right| = \left| {\left( {x - 1} \right)\left( {x - 2} \right)} \right| = \left\{ {\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}
 {\left( {x - 1} \right)\left( {x - 2} \right)}&{{\text{for}}}&{x 2} 
\end{array}} \right.$ 
and \[\cos \left( {\left| x \right|} \right) = \cos x\]
Therefore,
$f\left( x \right) = \left\{ {\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}
 {\left( {{x^2} - 1} \right)\left( {x - 1} \right)\left( {x - 2} \right) + \cos x}&{{\text{for}}}&{x 2} 
\end{array}} \right.$ 

Now, 
$f'\left( x \right) = \left\{ {\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}
 {2x\left( {x - 1} \right)\left( {x - 2} \right) + \left( {{x^2} - 1} \right)\left( {x - 2} \right) + \left( {{x^2} - 1} \right)\left( {x - 1} \right) - \sin x}&{{\text{for}}}&{x 2} 
\end{array}} \right.$ 

Clearly, 
$f'\left( {{1^ - }} \right) = - \sin 1 = f'\left( {{1^ + }} \right)$
So, f is differentiable at x = 1 and 
$f'\left( {{2^ - }} \right) = - \left( {4 - 1} \right)\left( {2 - 1} \right) - \sin 2 = - 3 - \sin 2$
and 
$f'\left( {{2^ + }} \right) = \left( {4 - 1} \right)\left( {2 - 1} \right) - \sin 2 = 3 - \sin 2$
Therefore, \[f'\left( {{2^ - }} \right) \ne f'\left( {{2^ + }} \right)\] and hence f is not differentiable at x = 2.

